{% from "macros.njk" import callout, snippet with context %}

{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>Text fields collect free-form input. The default is the outlined style
     (1px border, no fill); <code>fl-field--filled</code> switches to the M2
     filled style (tinted fill, flat bottom indicator) for dense forms. Both
     are plain HTML controls — the CSS only styles them, so autofill,
     validation and mobile keyboards all behave natively.</p>
  <h3>Do use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>You need <strong>single-line input</strong> (email, name, price,
        search) — a control inside a <code>fl-field__box</code> with a
        <code>fl-field__label</code> above.</li>
    <li>You need <strong>multi-line input</strong> — a real
        <code>&lt;textarea class="fl-field__input"&gt;</code>; it resizes
        vertically, or grows with content with the opt-in
        <code>fl-field__input--grow</code>.</li>
    <li>The form needs <strong>inline guidance or validation</strong> —
        <code>fl-field__help</code> on the footer line doubles as the error
        slot.</li>
    <li>The value needs <strong>units or context in the box</strong> — a
        prefix (<code>$</code>), a suffix (<code>lbs</code>, a domain), a
        leading search icon.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Don't use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>One value out of a known list — that is a
        <a href="select.html">select</a> or
        <a href="selection-controls.html">checkboxes/radios</a>; a text field
        invites typos you then have to validate away.</li>
    <li>You need a custom dropdown menu — the native control is the honest
        choice until the listbox work lands.</li>
    <li>The value is display-only — plain text, not a disabled field; disabled
        fields are for genuinely unavailable input.</li>
  </ul>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "Deliberately not floating labels",
    text: "Material 2 floats the label into the border notch. Flint keeps the label static above the field — the classic idiom, and M2's own \"text fields with independent labels\" pattern. It is the better accessibility shape (the label never doubles as a placeholder) and it is a permanent decision, not a missing feature. The 44px field height and 12px label follow from it.",
    href: "select.html",
    linkText: "Select — same static-label family"
  }) }}
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
  <div class="docs-anatomy">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 600 300" role="img" aria-label="Text field anatomy diagram">
      <rect x="1" y="1" width="598" height="298" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#dadce0"/>
      <!-- label -->
      <rect x="40" y="26" width="60" height="10" rx="5" fill="#444746"/>
      <!-- box: border, prefix, text, suffix -->
      <rect x="40" y="52" width="440" height="44" rx="4" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#dadce0"/>
      <rect x="58" y="68" width="14" height="12" rx="2" fill="#5f6368"/>
      <rect x="92" y="68" width="130" height="10" rx="5" fill="#1f1f1f"/>
      <rect x="428" y="68" width="34" height="10" rx="5" fill="#1f1f1f"/>
      <!-- footer: help left, counter right -->
      <rect x="40" y="106" width="150" height="8" rx="4" fill="#444746"/>
      <rect x="434" y="106" width="46" height="8" rx="4" fill="#444746"/>
      <!-- filled variant strip -->
      <rect x="40" y="140" width="440" height="44" fill="#e8eaed"/>
      <rect x="40" y="182" width="440" height="2" fill="#1f1f1f"/>
      <rect x="56" y="156" width="120" height="10" rx="5" fill="#1f1f1f"/>
      <!-- keylines -->
      <line x1="40" y1="52" x2="480" y2="52" stroke="#1a73e8" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/>
      <g font-family="system-ui, sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="700">
        <g><circle cx="28" cy="31" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="28" y="35" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">1</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="500" cy="74" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="500" y="78" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">2</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="76" cy="62" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="76" y="66" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">3</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="500" cy="110" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="500" y="114" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">4</text></g>
        <g><circle cx="28" cy="162" r="11" fill="#1a73e8"/><text x="28" y="166" fill="#ffffff" text-anchor="middle">5</text></g>
      </g>
    </svg>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Label</strong> — 12px/500 above the field; a real
          <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code>, so clicking it focuses the control.
          It never wraps the box (a label may not contain buttons, and helper
          text inside a label leaks into the accessible name).</li>
      <li><strong>Box</strong> — 44px tall, 1px <code>--fl-border</code>, 4px
          radius. The box owns every pixel of chrome: hover darkens the
          border, focus takes the accent border plus a real 2px outline,
          error recolors both. The control inside is borderless.</li>
      <li><strong>Affixes</strong> — <code>fl-field__prefix</code> /
          <code>fl-field__suffix</code>: 16px inside the border, in the value
          color (<code>--fl-text</code>), any width. Dimmed when the field is
          disabled.</li>
      <li><strong>Footer line</strong> — help left, character counter right
          (<code>fl-field__counter</code>, tabular numerals). The counter
          stays put when the help text swaps to an error message.</li>
      <li><strong>Filled variant</strong> — tinted <code>--fl-divider</code>
          fill, top corners only, flat bottom indicator (2px on focus via
          inset box-shadow — no full ring).</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-behavior">Behavior</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Structure is the API:</strong> <code>fl-field</code> is a
        <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> holding <code>fl-field__label</code> (a
        <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code>), a <code>fl-field__box</code>
        containing the control (and any affixes/icons), and an optional
        <code>fl-field__footer</code>. The box draws the border; the control
        is borderless inside it. That one structure covers plain fields,
        affixes, icons, selects and textareas.</li>
    <li><strong>Two ways to mark an error:</strong> add
        <code>fl-field--error</code> on the field, or set
        <code>aria-invalid="true"</code> on the control — either alone works
        and they stack. Either path recolors the border (or the filled
        indicator), the label and the help text. The attribute exists so JS
        validation code never has to know the class name.</li>
    <li><strong>Helper doubles as the error slot</strong> — reuse the same
        element and swap its text; the color follows the error state
        automatically. Link it with
        <code>aria-describedby</code> so screen readers announce it.</li>
    <li><strong>Affixes live inside the border</strong> at 16px from the
        edge, in the value color. They are plain siblings of the control, so
        any width works and a long value scrolls without running under
        them. Prefix or leading icon, suffix or trailing icon — don't
        combine both on one edge (M2 agrees).</li>
    <li><strong>Icons</strong> — <code>fl-field__icon</code> with
        <code>--leading</code> or <code>--trailing</code>. A decorative icon
        is a <code>&lt;span aria-hidden="true"&gt;</code>; an interactive one
        is a <code>&lt;button type="button"&gt;</code> (44px-wide target,
        hover fill, visible focus ring).</li>
    <li><strong>Password toggle</strong> — put
        <code>data-fl-password-toggle</code> on a trailing icon button
        pointing at the input with <code>aria-controls</code>. flint.js
        flips <code>password↔text</code>, restores the caret, swaps
        <code>aria-label</code> ("Show password"/"Hide password") and
        toggles an <code>is-revealed</code> class you can use to swap the
        eye icon. It is the only JavaScript in this component — without
        flint.js the field is a normal password input and the button does
        nothing.</li>
    <li><strong>Counter</strong> is author code: listen for
        <code>input</code> and write <code>value.length + "/" +
        maxlength</code> into <code>fl-field__counter</code>. Keep it
        <code>aria-hidden</code> — <code>maxlength</code> is what assistive
        tech needs.</li>
    <li><strong>Filled variant</strong> (<code>fl-field--filled</code>) keeps
        the same markup; only the box changes: tinted fill, top corners
        only, bottom indicator instead of a full border (2px on focus).</li>
    <li><strong>Textarea</strong> is <code>&lt;textarea
        class="fl-field__input"&gt;</code> — height auto, <code>rows</code>
        sets the initial size, resize is vertical-only. Add
        <code>fl-field__input--grow</code> to make it grow with content
        (<code>field-sizing: content</code>, capped at 240px; browsers
        without <code>field-sizing</code> keep the <code>rows</code>
        height).</li>
    <li><strong>Disabled</strong> is the native attribute — flat fill,
        dimmed value, affixes and icons; hover darkening switched off.
        Prefer removing optional guidance rather than dimming it.</li>
    <li><strong>A field without a visible label</strong> needs
        <code>aria-label</code> on the control (a search box with only a
        leading icon, for example).</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-accessibility">Accessibility</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The label is a real <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code></strong> —
        clicking it focuses the control and screen readers read it as the
        name. It is deliberately <em>not</em> a wrapper: a label containing
        a button is invalid HTML, and helper text inside a label can leak
        into the control's accessible name.</li>
    <li><strong>Link the help text</strong> with
        <code>aria-describedby</code>; it is the announcement for both
        guidance and error text.</li>
    <li><strong>Never color alone</strong> — errors are also
        <code>aria-invalid</code> plus visible error text; the color change
        is the third signal, not the only one.</li>
    <li><strong>Counter is <code>aria-hidden</code></strong> — announcing
        every keystroke is noise; the native <code>maxlength</code> is what
        assistive tech uses. If you want remaining characters announced, add
        your own <code>aria-live</code> region with sensible throttling.</li>
    <li><strong>Icon buttons must have names</strong> —
        <code>aria-label</code> on the toggle ("Show password"), and a
        meaningful name on any other interactive icon. Decorative icons get
        <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>The toggle's name describes the action</strong> and changes
        with the state ("Show password" ↔ "Hide password"). There is no
        <code>aria-pressed</code> — a changing name plus a pressed state
        announces contradictory information.</li>
    <li><strong>Never remove the focus style</strong> — the accent border
        plus the 2px outline is the only focus indicator the field has; it
        is a real <code>outline</code>, so it survives forced-colors mode
        where box-shadows do not.</li>
    <li><code>placeholder</code> is a hint, not a label — always ship a
        <code>fl-field__label</code> (or <code>aria-label</code>);
        placeholders disappear on input and are skipped by some screen
        readers.</li>
    <li>Disabled inputs are skipped by tab order — that is correct; if the
        field must stay discoverable, prefer <code>readonly</code> +
        explanation over <code>disabled</code>.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endset %}

